Great idea. I prefer having to get a tattoo artist to do it for you
than doing it yourself with a magic marker. A tatto artist patch would
well with the barber YANI posted a while back- the barber & tat artist
could be merged into one.

***Some spoilers ahead***


-Having a tattoo done should cause pain and maybe take off one or two
HP, depending on constitution.


-Occasionally tattoos get infected (especially in dirty, unhygenic
dungeons), so there should be a chance of getting ill or taking poison
damage after visiting the ink-shop.


-The tattoo artist should have lots of designs to offer, most of them
will have an effect but some will have none. Some tattoos:

--The name/image of your God: Helps appease angry deity, reduces your
normal prayer timeout slightly.

--The name/image of some other God from your class' pantheon: Not a
good idea, unless you were planning to convert.

--The name/image of any God from some other class' pantheon:
Unrestricts a few skills and tweaks your stats to make you more like
the other class, but you take a severe alignment hit, anger all Gods
of your own pantheon and risk losing some of your existing #enhance
potential. Maybe lose an XP level or two as well, and some negative
effect when you meet your quest leader.

--The name/image of your quest leader: YAFM message when you go on the
quest. No other effect.

--The name/image of your quest nemesis: You can't go on the quest
until you get the tattoo removed.

--The number 13: Boosts all stats by 1 but reduces your luck.

--The number 31: "Think you look pretty fly, don't you?" and reduce
charisma by 1. (Reference to the Offspring's "Pretty Fly For a White
Guy")

-- +1, +2...+7, -1, -2 etc: The modifier is applied to one random
wielded weapon or worn armour (stacked on top of any existing
modifiers). Every time the (w)ield, (W)ear or (T)ake off command is
successfully used the dice is rolled again and the modifier moved or
reapplied as appropriate. Over-enchantmant rules are ineffect, so
while a +4 tattoo is a great way to pump up your AC or fighting
abilities, its also a great way to accidentally evaporate that +2 SDSM
you just found on a bones pile.

--Any monster from the dungeon: Increases the chances of any
self-polymorph (poly control or not) resulting in the monster
depicted. This is a double-edged sword in itself, but just to make it
properly nasty (You wouldn't guess I was a Slashem player, would
you?), as long as you wear the tattoo you will automatically wake and
aggravate any monsters of the illustrated type.

--Any monster that a player cannot polymorph into: Just the
wake/aggravate as mentioned above (for consistency=-)

--Any named demon: While that demon is still alive, you get a major
stat boost. However, every turn you wear the tattoo and the named
demon is alive there is a 1 in 1000 chance of that demon being
summoned, hostile- even if it has been previously summoned peacefully.
If you kill the demon, your tattoo becomes useless and you lose your
stat boost. If you run for the stairs and ditch the demon on another
level, you keep the stats but sooner or later the tattoo will just
summon it again.

--Any of the four horsemen: Immunity to that horseman's attacks, but a
50% chance of that attack being effected out as soon as the tat is
complete: For example, getting a tattoo of Death will make you immune
to Death's touch when you meet him, but there's a 50/50 chance of
dropping dead in the artist's chair...

--Medusa: 5% chance of being turned to stone on completion of the
tattoo. 1% chance of any monsters that looks at you being petrified
(The tattoo artist charges triple for this one=-). THis one might be
too powerful, not sure.

--Croesus: No effect, except that vault guards suddenly know what he
looks like...

--Other named characters: No effect, other than YAFM when you meet
them.
--Scroll titles (ie ELBIB YLOH etc): That scroll's effect is carried
out once, with the B/U/C effect determined by the character's luck.
THis creates a new, interesting, expensive and potentially dangerous
(see below) way of IDing a scroll and finding out your luck status.

--Magic lamp: No effect, sorry. However this ought to be very tempting
for the unspoiled.

--Fountain: Ditto.



-Sometimes (around 10% of the time, modified by luck)  the tattoo
artist will get it wrong and ink something else of the same type. You
ask for "Anhur" and get "Thoth". Oops. You ask for a "13" but you get
a "31". You ask for Pestilence (because you can cast cure sickness)
and get Famine. You ask for ?identify and get ?amnesia. You ask for a
black dragon and get a grid bug.... I think you get the idea.

-I definitely agree that there should only be space for one tattoo per
body, and I think they should be very hard and/or dangerous to remove
so you have to be very careful about which tattoo you choose, and make
sure the artist gets it right (pay him extra?).

--Bathing in a fountain/ getting splashed with water to remove a
tattoo is too easy, imnsho.

--Getting splashed with acid is good, but only if the character does
NOT have acid resistance.

--I don't know of any lasers in nethack, but Slashem has lightsabers,
and I can imagine they would do the trick very effectively- and
painfully.

--Logically, stripping naked and reading destroy armour ought to do
the trick, but that seems pretty easy as well. Maybe there should be
some kind of HP penalty attached to this? ("Your feel as though you're
being flayed alive!").

--Perhaps certain polymorphs might do the trick as well- polys into
insubstantial forms like fog clouds, vortices and so on. It might be
fun to make mold, lichen and acid blob polymorphs a sure way to remove
tattoos. I can just see some XPlvl 20 Valk getting splatted by a wimpy
ilttle creature after taking lichen form to get rid a botched
tattoo...

